.\" Man page header metadata (optional, but good practice)
.\" These lines are often added by tooling but help structure the file.
.TH obc 1 10 Oct 2023 obc 1.0 \"Oberon-to-C Transpiler\"
.SH NAME
obc - Oberon-to-C Transpiler
.SH SYNOPSIS
obc [options] <main.mod> ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
obc is a transpiler designed to convert source code written in the Oberon 
language into standard C source code. It handles multi-module projects 
by automatically traversing dependencies, compiling all required modules, 
and linking the resulting C files into a single executable binary.

The core philosophy of obc is to treat Oberon modules as first-class 
citizens, generating both primary source files (.c) and necessary 
header files (.h) for library modules.

.PP MODULES.
obc expects the main module name as the last positional argument. All 
required dependencies should be available on the module search path 
(see options).

.SH OPTIONS
.var
-o OUTFILE
    Specify the name of the final output binary executable. 
    If omitted, the binary name is derived from the main 
    module file name (with the extension removed).

.var
--emit-c
    If specified, obc will leave the generated .c and 
    .h source files in the current directory instead of 
    deleting them upon successful compilation.

.var
--warnings
    Enable stricter C compiler warning flags (e.g., -Wall) 
    during the final linking stage. This is generally 
    recommended for library code.

.var
--mod-path DIR1:DIR2: ...
    Sets the module search path for all subsequent 
    module lookups. Modules can be found in any directory 
    listed, separated by colons (:).

.var
-I DIR
    A shorthand for --mod-path. Specifies an additional 
    include directory for module resolution. Multiple -I 
    flags can be used.

.var
OBC_MOD_PATH
    Environment variable that sets the default module search path. 
    This variable acts identically to --mod-path and is used 
    if no module path is provided via the command line.

.SH EXAMPLES
To compile a simple main program named `my_app` requiring the 
`strings` and `filesystem` libraries, and outputting an executable 
named `my_program`:

.nf
obc --mod-path /opt/modules -o my_program my_app.mod
.fi

To compile and explicitly keep the generated source files:

.nf
obc --emit-c my_main.mod
.fi

To include a custom library directory and enable strict warnings:

.nf
obc -I /usr/local/lib/mods --warnings main.mod
.fi

.SH SEE ALSO
Makefile(1), gcc(1), preprocessor(1)

.SH NOTES
.b
.b1 Dependency Order: obc operates in two passes. First, it detects 
all user-defined dependencies recursively. All detected modules 
are compiled into C source files before the main module is processed.
.b2 Compilation Process: The final executable is built by calling 
`gcc` with all generated `.c` files and necessary include 
paths (`-I`). Linking usually requires `-lm` (math) and `-lz` 
(compression).
.b3 Module Resolution: obc searches for a module named `ModName` 
in the following order: 
a) In the directory of the calling module.
b) In the current working directory.
c) In any directory specified by --mod-path or -I.
.end
